Claim 12 - Nightwatch (what it is, whether it is implemented enough
                       to claim publicly)
=====================================================================

Method: Static inspection only. No CLI / live tests executed.

Findings:

1. Nightwatch is a real, registered cron job, not a label:
   - src/cron/nightwatch.ts:30-37 (`NIGHTWATCH_DEFAULTS` with cron
     expression `0 3 * * *`, default repo path, timezone; also
     NIGHTWATCH_JOB_NAME = "nightwatch-daily")
   - src/cron/nightwatch.ts:727-775 (registerNightwatchJob: registers
     or replaces the cron job inside the CronService; handles
     duplicates by removing them; respects nightwatchCfg.enabled)

2. It has a defined runtime behavior - daily code review of the
   repo, delivered to Telegram:
   - src/cron/nightwatch.ts:594-655 (buildNightwatchPrompt builds the
     review prompt; includes a self-maintenance section telling the
     review to update its own key-file list when files are renamed)
   - src/cron/nightwatch.ts:658-725 (runNightwatch: skips if no
     telegram chat / token is configured, skips if no git changes
     since the last run, otherwise builds the prompt, runs the goal
     planner via handleGoal, and delivers the plan to the configured
     Telegram chat/topic)
   - src/cron/nightwatch.ts:668-676 (best-effort lesson condensation
     runs alongside nightwatch but never blocks it)

3. It is configurable from Telegram with a real command surface, so
   the token is user-facing, not internal-only:
   - src/telegram/nightwatch-commands.ts:17-22
     (NIGHTWATCH_COMMAND_SPECS describes the `/nightwatch` Telegram
     command with the description "Configure nightwatch daily code
     review schedule")
   - src/telegram/nightwatch-commands.ts:24-32 (`NIGHTWATCH_USAGE`
     advertises `/nightwatch`, `/nightwatch on`, `/nightwatch off`,
     `/nightwatch time HH:MM`, `/nightwatch tz Area/City`,
     `/nightwatch chat` to the operator)
   - src/telegram/bot-native-commands.ts:54-56 (the command specs are
     imported alongside the other native Telegram commands)
   - src/telegram/bot-native-commands.ts:174,185 (nightwatch command
     specs are concatenated into the public command list)
   - src/telegram/bot-native-commands.ts:530-533 (the command is
     registered as a real Telegram handler at startup)

4. It is wired into gateway startup, with tests, so this is not a
   sketch:
   - src/gateway/server-cron.ts (registers nightwatch as part of
     cron setup; confirmed by tests below)
   - src/gateway/server-cron.nightwatch.test.ts (dedicated
     integration test for nightwatch within the cron service)
   - src/cron/nightwatch.test.ts (unit tests for the module)
   - src/telegram/nightwatch-commands.test.ts (unit tests for the
     command surface)

Implementation summary:
- "Nightwatch" is a daily cron-triggered Telegram-delivered review
  that asks the goal planner to scan the repository's recent
  activity and produce a review/follow-up plan.
- It is configurable end-to-end from Telegram, persists schedule
  state, condenses lessons as a side-effect, and is integrated into
  the gateway's cron service.
- The token "nightwatch" is user-facing: it appears as the literal
  Telegram command `/nightwatch` and as the visible description
  "nightwatch daily code review".

Safe README wording:
- "Nightwatch runs a scheduled daily code review against the
  repository and delivers the resulting goal plan to a configured
  Telegram chat. Schedule, timezone, and target chat are
  configurable through the `/nightwatch` command."

Unsafe wording to avoid:
- "Nightwatch fixes bugs overnight" - it delivers a plan; execution
  still goes through the normal goal approval flow.
- "Always-on monitoring of production" - the trigger is cron + git
  changes, not live telemetry.

STATUS: verified
REASON: Nightwatch is a real cron job (`nightwatch-daily`) with a
registered handler in src/cron/nightwatch.ts, a user-facing
`/nightwatch` Telegram command in src/telegram/nightwatch-commands.ts,
gateway-wiring tests, and a unit-test suite. The token is
explicitly user-facing in Telegram, so it is safe to use in the
README provided the wording stays close to "scheduled daily code
review delivered to Telegram".
